<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Acceptance criterion C4.

  `android:usesCleartextTraffic="false"` on <application> is the coarse switch; this file is the
  one that survives a manifest merge, because a library that declares its own
  networkSecurityConfig loses to the application's. Both are set for that reason.

  WHY cleartext matters more here than on a typical app: the traffic is a Supabase session
  token, evidence photographs, and a signed compliance record. The network is a contractor's
  phone on whatever Wi-Fi the site offers, which on a construction or industrial site is
  routinely an open guest SSID. Cleartext there is not theoretical.

  cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" with NO per-domain exception. There is no debug-only
  localhost exception either: a `debug-overrides` block applies to any build the tooling marks
  debuggable, and the temptation to point the app at a plain-HTTP dev server is exactly how an
  exception outlives the afternoon it was added. `flutter run` talks to the app over adb, not
  over HTTP, so nothing in development needs one.

  System CA store only. No user-installed certificates are trusted, so an interception proxy
  whose root has been added on the device cannot read the session token — this is the default
  from API 24 onward and is stated explicitly so it cannot be silently widened.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
